  "slug": "automated-microscopy-2e31",
  "summary": "Preprints and one published framework now package virtual staining, single-molecule, and spatial transcriptomics analyses into quantitative computational tools.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is software that converts raw imaging or spatial data into quantitative, benchmarked measurements. DynaCell provides a benchmark for 3D time-lapse virtual staining of live cells with baselines and three metric tiers, while QUEST virtually stains H&E histology to predict multiplex immunofluorescence protein expression across scales. PixAQ applies marker-specific probabilistic pixel attribution to single-cell protein quantification in imaging mass cytometry, and CardioShift models nested variance to resolve single-cell Ca2+ transient and contractility heterogeneity in hiPSC-cardiomyocytes. MaCADEMIA packages single-molecule localisation analyses into a MATLAB toolbox. In spatial transcriptomics, TileScope quantifies spatial relationships among genes and programs, Tracel reassigns transcripts to cells via optimal transport, and SWIMR integrates multiple scRNA-seq references for deconvolution. A shared feature is that attribution, heterogeneity, and evaluation are built into the tools themselves.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-04"
}
